ILL 2319 ji lta ba de ltar du, mi sdug pa'i yid can gyi rnal 'byor pa ltar cig shos te rnal 'byor pa ma yin pas, rnal 'byor bas keng rus mthong ba'i yul der blo gtad pa ste keng rus la sogs pa'i yul la lta 'dod pa rnams la yang keng rus rtogs pa ste mthong ba skye bar 'gyur te, sngon po sogs kyi rnam shes bzhin no Just so—in the same way—we can have a case where there is a certain other individual who is not a deep practitioner; which is to say, they are not like the deep practitioner whose mind has taken on an aspect of the repulsive. It should be the case then that if they decide they would like to look upon the skeletons (or whatever else it may be)—if they were to direct their minds towards the same objects where the deep practitioner perceives skeletons—then a perception of the skeletons, that sight, should also arise in them. It would be just the same as the perception of blue and the like.
ILL 2320 'dir 'grel pa dang bstun na, ,khyod kyi dbang blo'i yul gtad ji lta ba, ,de ltar mi sdug yid can de bzhin du, ,yul der blo gtad cig shos rnams la yang, ,skye 'gyur de ni brdzun par yang mi 'gyur, zhes pa'i 'gyur cig thob par snang ngo If we were to match these thoughts up to the autocommentary at this point, then it would seem that we could come up with a translation of the same lines as follows— You direct your awareness Of the powers of sense In a certain way, And they take on thus A certain aspect; Just so, When the mind is directed Towards a certain object, Then it should arise For the other ones as well; Neither would this be Deceptive.
ILL 2321 ting nge 'dzin 'di brdzun par te log pa'i don yid la byed par yang mi 'gyur ro Neither is it the case that this meditative concentration would be deceptive; which is to say, you would not be directing your mind towards something mistaken.
ILL 2322 rnal 'byor pa yin min mgo mtshungs mdzad pa yang, shes pa rang bzhin gyis grub pa yin na keng rus kyi man ngag sgom pa la ltos pa 'gog nus pa'i gnad kyis yin no This is true because of a crucial point here. Even if we were to say that the two observers were the same—in either being a deep practitioner, or not—in a case where their states of mind existed through some nature of their own, we would be able to deny that they were relying on a meditation upon the advices for the practice of the skeletons.
ILL 2323 [vi.71a] ,rab rib dang ldan dbang po can mtshungs la, ,chu 'bab rlung {%klung} la yi dags {%dvags} rnag blo yang For cases equivalent To a state of mind With a sense power That has cataracts, There is also The state of mind Of pus for those Craving spirits Towards a stream Of fresh water.
ILL 2324 de bzhin du rab rib dang ldan pa'i dbang po can dang mtshungs pa'i sgyu ma dang gzugs brnyan sogs dper 'god pa la yang lan gdab tshul shes par bya la, chu 'bab pa'i klung la yi dvags rnams la rnag khrag gi blo skye ba la yang snga ma bzhin du shes par bya'o You can understand, in the same way, how to reply as well to cases where someone sets forth—as an example—an illusion, or a reflection in a mirror, as being equivalent to a state of mind with a sense power that has cataracts. And you should also understand, in the same way, the illustration of when a state of mind arises in craving spirits wherein they perceive a stream of fresh water as being pus & blood.
ILL 2325 de la chos kyi skye mched kyi gzugs lnga ni dngos por smra ba'i grub mthas btags pa min gyi, mdo las gsungs shing rang nyid kyang de ltar du bzhed do Now the five types of form relating to the sense door of all things are not something simply imputed by the school of thought known as the Functionalists. Rather, they have been stated in sutra, and we also accept them as being such.
ILL 2326 de'i phyir keng rus med kyang de gsal bar snang ba ni, gzugs brnyan dang 'dra bar gzugs can du 'dod dgos la, 'di yid shes kho na la snang ba yin pas, gzugs brnyan bzhin du gzugs kyi skye mched min la, skye mched gzugs can lhag ma dgu yang min pas chos kyi skye mched kyi gzugs kun brtags pa zhes 'byung ba'o As such, even though the skeletons don't really exist, we do have to accept that the vivid aspect of them does exist; and that it involves form, in the same way that a reflection in the mirror does. And so despite the fact that the image of the skeletons is not, like the reflection in a mirror, something possessed of form, neither does it belong to any of the remaining nine doors of sense of form. And this means that it constitutes form within the sense door of all things, and can be referred to as an imaginary form.
